en maybe, I don't know how you do the the pattern trend where you like, if you identify the same uh, kind of direction and step, like up or down, the direction of the, the, the delta from, so take take one pixel and go in any direction and calculate the the delta, the the difference in color.\n\n32. In all of its properties, using hue, saturation, and brightness is, you know, pretty a logical choice.\n\n33. And anyway, so you, you, you compare the delta, but then you want to compare the, the deltas for all the, uh, I don't know if it has to be the exact same direction, because like, yeah, I, I don't know how you do that regional analysis to see see the patterns, like a gradient, right?\n\n34. A gradient is sort of like a continuous flow of color and so measuring the actual delta is important and so I mean it's sort of like a derivative right it's taking the derivative of the rate of change of the image from one pixel to the next but it I, I do think the direction matters so I'm not sure exactly the best way to um to describe that but it's sort of like yeah the The, when when you're drawing a gradient in any 2D program, you, you have to specify the coordinates of the gradient and like the direction and, and the speed at which it flows and whatever.\n\n35. So that's kind of what we're doing here.\n\n36. Um, if you can write a, an image filter that uh, you know you can apply to any image, so you input an image and you get a uh, grayscale, but mostly you know very much white and black, I think.\n\n37. I don't know if there's a use case.\n\n38. Maybe surfaces could be some sort of grayscale, but I don't know if I want to uh, start coloring shit in yet.\n\n39. It's mostly just, you know, to see the the line art, to see the linear calculation, and, um, well, and then I don't know.\n\n40. We could theorize about how to efficiently turn that into three dimensional space.\n\n41. I do think just trying to ballpark rough. like heights and widths and uh well and coordinates f- f