+      "text": "Base directory for this skill: C:\\Users\\mike\\.claude\\skills\\fans\n\n# Fans\n\nThe usual suspect is `node server.js` — the lew42 dev server — pinned at ~130% while\nstill serving (`c:\\Code\\lew42\\monorepo\\Server\\doc\\spin.md`: cause unconfirmed, comes\nback every ~4 days). Second suspect: a `claude.exe` in the upstream idle busy-wait\n(anthropics/claude-code #10493 #30807 #22275). Never assume — sample.\n\n## 0. Ask Servex first (2026-09-30)\n\nFirst, `GET http://127.0.0.1:8090/api/processes`: `groups[]` gives CPU % and RAM per task and per Claude Code session, `orphans[]` lists leftover waiters (bash, tail, grep, sleep) and whether Servex already ended them. Servex reaps idle orphans itself and removes finished worktrees; what is left for you is what it does not own. Detail: `Servex/doc/processes.md`. If Servex is down, go to step 1.\n\n## 1. Sample — who burns *now*\n\n```powershell\n$p = @(Get-Process | ? { $_.CPU -gt 0 }); $a = @{}; $p | % { $a[$_.Id] = $_.CPU }; sleep 4\n$p | % { try { $_.Refresh(); [pscustomobject]@{ pid=$_.Id; name=$_.ProcessName; core=[int](($_.CPU-$a[$_.Id])/4*100); since=$_.StartTime; mb=[int]($_.WorkingSet64/1MB) } } catch {} } | sort core -desc | select -first 8 | ft -auto\n```\n\nThen, for anything over ~50%: `Get-CimInstance Win32_Process -Filter \"ProcessId=<pid>\" | select CommandLine, ParentProcessId`.\n\n## 2. Name it\n\n- `node.exe server.js`, parent gone → the dev server. Read `Server/doc/spin.md`; do step 3 before step 4.\n- `claude.exe --output-format stream-json …`, parent `Code.exe` → a VS Code extension conversation. There are typically 10–15, alive for days, 300–500 MB each — **not abandoned minions**: the extension keeps one per conversation started since VS Code last launched (2026-08-18: all 13 post-dated the 8/16 09:11 `Code.exe`; whether closing a tab reaps it is unverified). Idle at 0–4% they cost RAM only; ignore. Fork workers (`claude --resume … --fork-session -p`) run in print mode and exit when done — none should be found, and none ever have been.\n- `node.exe` under `Adobe` or `@playwright/mcp` → leave it.\n- Anything else → look before you kill; the pattern-match may be wrong.\n\n## 3. Evidence before the kill (node only)\n\n```powershell\nnode ~/.claude/skills/fans/profile.mjs <pid> 5\n```\n\nPrints the hottest frames and writes `<pid>.cpuprofile` in the cwd (run it from the\nscratchpad). Paste the top frames into the answer. `(idle) 100%` means it is not busy in JS:\nlook at handles/threads instead. Profiled once (2026-08-19): **the spin is the two chokidar\nwatchers flooded by raw `fs.watch` events** — main thread in `handleEvent`, threadpool in\nreaddir — not idle, not a retry loop. What floods them is the open question, so also run\n\n```powershell\nnode C:\\Users\\mike\\.claude\\skills\\fans\\watchspy.mjs 10    # from the repo root; events/s + top paths\n```\n\nThousands per second = those paths are the cause; paste them into `Server/doc/spin.md`.\n(`~` does not expand for node under PowerShell — use the full path.) Reads count on this\nbox: the first data read of a file with a >1 h-stale atime fires two change events.\n\n## 4. Kill — spare yourself\n\nYour own session: `$PID` → parent is your `claude.exe`. Never in the list.\n\n- `node`: `Stop-Process -Id <pid> -Force`. Dev server → **do not restart it hidden** —\n  the owner runs `node server.js` in a terminal of their own (2026-08-18: \"i'd rather see\n  it in my term\"; a hidden one nobody can find, and its console output is the evidence\n  nobody has). Say port 80 is free. If asked to start it: `Start-Process node server.js\n  -WorkingDirectory c:\\Code\\lew42\\monorepo` (visible window), then say the pid.\n- `claude.exe`: killing them in bulk trips the permission classifier — hand over the\n  `Stop-Process -Id a,b,c -Force` line. A killed session is resumable: `claude --resume <id>`.\n\n## 5. The janitor already patrols\n\n`~/.claude/janitor/janitor.ps1`, scheduled task **Claude Janitor**, every\n15 min: any `claude`/`node` >12h old, not Adobe, sustaining >90% across two consecutive\nruns → killed, logged in `janitor.log` beside it (`state.json` = strike 1). Read the log\nfirst — it may have already acted (and it does not restart the dev server). Verified no\nfalse positives since 2026-08-07.\n\n## 6. Report\n\nA short table (pid · what · core% · since), what was killed/restarted, the profile's top\nframe if there was one, and what's still unknown. Then update `Server/doc/spin.md` if\nthe profile finally named the cause.\n"
